  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 131 AM CDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nA decaying boundary is currently positioned over northern portions \nof the area with southerly flow observed over the Coastal Plains and \nlight northerly or light and variable winds over northern areas. \nMoisture-pooling along this boundary is also noted in satellite PWAT \nestimates which peak in the 2.2 inch range near the Victoria \nCrossroads. This boundary will remain over the today while a \nweakening mid-lvl vort max will shift southward into the area this \nmorning. There will be about a 20-25 percent chances of showers and \nstorms with this initial AM impulse (focused over the northern third \nof the area), with a secondary round (roughly 25-30% chance) of \nstorms may develop further south later this afternoon as the sea \nbreeze interacts with the aforementioned boundary. Although I don't \nexpect storm coverage to be more than scattered, inverted V profiles \nwith DCAPE values over 1200 J/kg will support some stronger \ndownbursts, and wouldn't even totally rule out a rogue severe gust. \nThe boundary will wash out by Monday, with PWATs dropping to below \nclimo for the remainder of the week as more consistent southeasterly \nlow-lvl flow returns. Given the influence of the H5 ridge to our \nwest and the lack of any appreciable mid-lvl disturbances, rain \nchances will be below 15% through the workweek, although there will \nbe low chances of sea breeze activity returning in the latter half \nof the week. \n\nAlthough increased cloud cover today will likely result in slightly \ncooler conditions than Saturday's scorcher we will remain seasonably \nwarm through the forecast period with high temperatures largely in \nthe triple digits west of I-77, and 90s elsewhere.\nThese temperatures combined with lows barely clearing 80 degrees \nwill maintain a moderate to major heat risk each day. Max heat \nindices will also flirt with heat advisory criteria each day Monday \nthrough Thursday and short-fused advisories will need to be \nconsidered. Finally, for anyone doing strenuous activities in direct \nsunlight, WBGT values will peak in the high to extreme range, \noccasionally even exceeding 90 degrees. To sum it all up, make sure \nto stay hydrated, take frequent cooling breaks, and seek shade when \npossible as the dog days of summer are upon us in South Texas.",
